<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Stalingrad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a>–<a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a> was fought from August 1942 to February 1943 for the city of Stalingrad, now Volgograd, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. After German forces took most of the city in brutal street fighting, the Soviet Operation Uranus in November 1942 encircled the Sixth Army, whose remnants surrendered under Friedrich Paulus on 2 February 1943. With casualties of around two million in all, it marked a turning point of the war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-stalingrad.jpg" alt="Battle of Stalingrad" width="330" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Расчет_советской_76,2-мм_пушки_ЗиС-3_ведет_огонь_в_Сталинграде._Ноябрь_1942_г.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the spring of 1942 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and the Baltic republics, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> chose the south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> as the target of the summer campaign. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s fuel supplies had dwindled, and the plan, code-named Fall Blau, aimed to seize the Soviet oil fields in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. Stalingrad was one of the largest industrial centres of the Soviet Union and a transport hub on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga River</a>, and controlling it would block Soviet shipments along the river. The city also carried the name of the Soviet leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>. Hitler split the army group, sending one part south to the Caucasus and the other, including Paulus's 6th Army, east towards the Volga.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>German forces first met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>'s Stalingrad Front on 17 July 1942. On 23 August the 6th Army and part of the 4th Panzer Army began their offensive, supported by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> bombing that reduced much of the city to rubble. The fighting became house-to-house. The Soviet commander Chuikov kept his front line as close to the Germans as possible, so that German artillery and aircraft could not strike without risking their own men, and used small units moving through the city's sewers. After 27 September the struggle moved to the factory district, and by November the Germans held 90 per cent of the ruined city, with the Soviet defenders confined to two narrow pockets on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>'s west bank.</p><p>On 19 November the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> launched Operation Uranus against the Romanian armies guarding the German flanks. The flanks were overrun and about 330,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> troops were trapped. Hitler forbade a breakout; attempts to supply the army by air failed, and Manstein's relief attempt, Operation Winter Storm, did not reach it. On 7 January 1943 Soviet envoys offered terms of surrender, which Hitler refused to let Paulus accept. On 2 February 1943 the 6th Army, out of ammunition and food, capitulated, the first of Hitler's field armies to do so.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> suffered between 800,000 and 1,500,000 casualties, killed, wounded or captured, among the German armed forces and their allies. Of the troops trapped in the pocket, 105,000 eventually surrendered and 60,000 died. The German public was told of the disaster only at the end of January 1943; on 31 January state radio broadcast the Adagio of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner">Anton Bruckner</a>'s Seventh Symphony before announcing the defeat. On 18 February Joseph Goebbels gave his Sportpalast speech calling for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/total-war">total war</a>.</p><p>The defeat ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s 1942 summer offensive and passed the strategic initiative on the Eastern Front to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. It is generally regarded as the turning point of the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Stalingrad was named a Hero City in 1945, and the monument The Motherland Calls was erected on Mamayev Kurgan in 1967.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Stalingrad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The support cost the Hungarians dearly: almost the entire Second Army of the Royal Hungarian Army was lost at the <b>Battle of Stalingrad</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeat at the <b>Battle of Stalingrad</b>, where the Sixth Army surrendered in early 1943, and the failed offensive at the Battle of Kursk that July lost the initiative in the east.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Second World War it bore much of the fighting after Germany invaded on 22 June 1941: Leningrad endured a siege of almost 900 days, the Germans were stopped before Moscow in December 1941, and the battles of <b>Stalingrad</b> in 1942–1943 and Kursk in 1943 turned the war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-sfsr">Russian SFSR · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…catastrophic losses, but the Red Army halted the Germans at Moscow in December 1941, destroyed an entire German army at <b>Stalingrad</b> in 1942–1943, won the tank battle at Kursk in July 1943 and smashed Army Group Centre in Operation Bagration in 1944, before taking Berlin in May 1945.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Military</a></li></ul></section>
